{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787458125,"nonce":"67R1fcJeOQZPg0yc4W9/Ig+t3UY=","title":"`concurrency_slots.group_key` is a global primary key fed an author-controlled","message":"string with no tenant prefix, so two unrelated orgs collide — and under\n`cancel-in-progress` one org can cancel another org's Run.\n\nWhere:\n- `crates/scarab-db-postgres/migrations/0008_concurrency.sql:14` —\n  `CREATE TABLE concurrency_slots (group_key TEXT PRIMARY KEY, holder ...)`.\n  Globally unique, no org/project column.\n- `crates/scarab-server/src/lib.rs:4187-4199` — the author's `concurrency.group`\n  string is passed to `set_run_concurrency` verbatim, unprefixed.\n- `crates/scarab-db-postgres/src/lib.rs:729-762` — `acquire_slot` binds it raw.\n- `crates/scarab-engine/src/scheduler.rs:1454-1456` — on contention with\n  `CancelInProgress`, the incoming Run cancels the current `holder`.\n\nWhy this is a defect and not a choice: the adjacent `supersede_key` IS\nrepo-prefixed — `crates/scarab-server/src/lib.rs:3400` builds\n`format!(\"{}/{}:{scope}:{pipeline}\", repo.owner, repo.name)`. Two sibling keys,\none scoped and one not, is an oversight rather than a decision.\n\nFailure scenario: org A and org B both author `concurrency: { group: deploy }`.\nUnconditionally, their Runs serialise against each other — B's deploy waits on\nA's, across a tenant boundary, with no way for either to see why. If either\npipeline also sets `cancel-in-progress`, B's Run actively cancels A's.\n\nSeverity nuance worth keeping: ADR-0032 forces environment-targeting (governed)\nRuns to `Queue`, so the cross-tenant *cancel* can only fire between ungoverned\nruns. The cross-tenant *serialisation* is unconditional.\n\nFix: prefix the group key with the run's tenant the same way `supersede_key`\ndoes. Needs a migration (existing rows carry unprefixed keys) and a decision on\nwhether the grain is org or org/project.","files":null}]}